{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2505 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "708d55be50a2812b34bbd89ea0649400ff1822b138ea821045fec0ba55d257e1" :edb/db-before-t 2504 :edb/db-after-t 2505 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535817 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:02:23.807Z" 13194139535817 true] [17592186096546 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368066 13194139535817 true] [17592186096547 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368073 13194139535817 true] [17592186096893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535817 true] [17592186096893 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/assets/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139535817 true] [17592186096893 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1046/Source/Images/q-114418.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = g^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139535817 false] [17592186096893 1145 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ no inflection points." 13194139535817 true] [17592186294077 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070683/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139535817 true] [17592186294077 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" 13194139535817 false] [17592186368066 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368067 13194139535817 true] [17592186368066 1089 "q-21034" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368066 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535817 true] [17592186368066 1100 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x-12.$" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368066 1113 #uuid "25002641-d57a-5f83-8d24-8844df9e689a" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368066 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n6x+6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-2$ and $x=0$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+6=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =6(0)+6=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368068 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368069 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368070 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368071 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368072 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1041 "selection" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368068 13194139535817 true] [17592186368067 1110 #uuid "192b540d-114b-5dca-93b2-0011a1a2c50f" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368068 1034 "x=-1" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535817 true] [17592186368068 1111 #uuid "3de3318e-ac3e-5b9e-bd98-3c3c30362ac0" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368069 1034 "x=1" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535817 true] [17592186368069 1111 #uuid "370e82fa-244f-565d-bd7b-686c93e1813e" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368070 1034 "x=-3" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368070 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535817 true] [17592186368070 1111 #uuid "0e7ee85f-00db-53c2-8192-7e71a85b178c" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368071 1034 "x=2" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368071 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535817 true] [17592186368071 1111 #uuid "46b5cc84-9763-5021-84f4-eb5ec4cfedf1" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368072 1034 "x=-2" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535817 true] [17592186368072 1111 #uuid "3a8ad7ad-42bc-5b39-aa84-d6c8a4355b3e" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368073 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368074 13194139535817 true] [17592186368073 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368076 13194139535817 true] [17592186368073 1089 "q-344301" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368073 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535817 true] [17592186368073 1100 "In **ascending order**,the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535817 true] [17592186368073 1113 #uuid "1ead159f-d5ec-5c5c-a0d2-cbb1f6821732" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368073 1145 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)+{e}^{x}(2x-12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22)+{e}^{x}(2x-10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=1,\\,$ $\\,x=4,\\,$ and $\\,x=7,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1-2)(1-6))=5e>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4-2)(4-6))=-4{e}^{4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(7) & ={e}^{7}((7-2)(7-6))=5{e}^{7}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2$ and $x=6$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6,$ we conclude that $x=2$ and $x=6$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=2$ and $x=6$ are inflection points." 13194139535817 true] [17592186368074 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368075 13194139535817 true] [17592186368074 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535817 true] [17592186368074 1041 "field-1" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368074 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368075 13194139535817 true] [17592186368074 1110 #uuid "fab84f36-7c4f-50fb-9534-d25126e51804" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368075 1034 "2" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535817 true] [17592186368075 1111 #uuid "2cfdd7cf-fbda-5a4f-b854-4493e97c69f1" 13194139535817 true] [17592186368076 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368077 13194139535817 true] [17592186368076 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535817 true] [17592186368076 1041 "field-2" 13194139535817 true] [1759